        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target:** `F:ml430-nat-bitwise-bit-4c4b28a8` — Mathlib's `Nat.bitwise_bit'`\n\n**Formal Statement:**\n```lean\n∀ {f : Bool → Bool → Bool} (a : Bool) (m : ℕ) (b : Bool) (n : ℕ),\n  (m = 0 → a = true) → (n = 0 → b = true) → \n  Nat.bitwise f (Nat.bit a m) (Nat.bit b n) = Nat.bit (f a b) (Nat.bitwise f m n)\n```\n\n**Structural Shape:** Universal quantification over a boolean function `f` and two bit-representations (`Nat.bit a m`, `Nat.bit b n`), with preconditions ensuring well-formedness (zero implies true bit), concluding with an equality showing bitwise operations distribute over the bit constructor.\n\n**Dependencies:** None (no upstream dependencies).\n\n**Would Unlock:** `F:ml430-nat-bitwise-swap-7175e90e`\n\n**Premises Available:** The nat prelude contains 249 theorems total, but **no theorems matching `Nat.bitwise*` or `Nat.bit*`** — the lookup succeeded but returned zero matches. This means the kernel has no direct bitwise operation lemmas.\n\n**Registry Status:** No registered operations target this fact.\n\n**Similar Facts:** The frontier contains 98 eligible facts, mostly Int/Nat number theory (modular arithmetic, gcd, coprimality, factorials). No other bitwise-related facts visible in the sample.\n\n**Challenge:** Proving bitwise distribution without bitwise premises in the kernel.",
